course: dictionary
lesson: 6870
title: Words that spill over
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Overflow is a word with more than one meaning, so the words around it show you which meaning is right. «If the bath overflows, turn off the tap.» Water runs over the edge because the bath is too full, so to overflow means to flow over the edge.
explain: «The hall was so crowded that the crowd overflowed onto the steps.» There were too many people to fit inside, so overflowed means there was more than the space could hold. Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
explain: Look at whether the word is doing something or is a thing. In «overflowing with joy», it describes a feeling that is so big it seems to spill out. In «a pipe or hole that lets extra water out», an overflow is the thing itself. In maths, a 2-litre jug given 3 litres lets 1 litre overflow.
fact: Overflow means to flow over the edge because something is too full.
fact: «If a bath or sink overflows, turn off the tap, pull the plug, and tell a grown-up.»
fact: Most baths and sinks have an overflow hole that lets extra water out.
fact: In computing, an overflow is a number too big for the space it is stored in. In one byte, 255 plus 1 rolls over to 0.
exercise: «A bath left running can overflow and damage floors and the ceiling below.» What does overflow mean here? | Turn cold and freeze | Go very slowly and quietly | Shrink smaller | * Flow over the edge because it is too full
exercise: «The hall was so crowded that the crowd overflowed onto the steps.» What does overflowed mean? | Stayed inside, neatly seated | Went quietly and slowly | Became smaller and tidier | * Spilled out beyond the space, because there were too many
exercise: Maya is overflowing with joy after her win. What does overflowing with joy mean? | She is cold and wet | * She has a lot of joy, so much it seems to spill out | She has no joy at all | She is stuck in the water
test: «A 2-litre jug is given 3 litres of water. How much overflows?» | 5 litres | 2 litres | 3 litres | * 1 litre
test: «If the sink might overflow, why should you turn off the tap?» | To let the sink fill up more | To make the sink colder | * To stop water spilling and flooding the floor | To make a loud noise
exam: «In one byte, 255 plus 1 rolls over to 0, a bug called an overflow.» What is an overflow in this sentence? | A pipe that lets extra water out of a bath | * A number too big for the space it is stored in | A very small number that disappears | Water spilling over the edge of a bath
